      "First the browser parses your HTML into the **DOM** (Document Object Model) — a tree of objects, one per tag, that code and styles can attach to. It parses your CSS into a parallel tree, the **CSSOM** (CSS Object Model), which knows every rule. It combines the two into a **render tree**: only the things that will actually be drawn (a `display: none` element is in the DOM but not the render tree).",
      "Then comes **layout** (also called *reflow*): the browser walks the render tree and computes the exact size and position of every box — how wide this paragraph is, where that button sits. **Paint** turns each box into actual pixels (colours, text, shadows, borders) on layers. Finally **composite** stacks those layers in the right order and hands the result to the screen.",
      "The two words worth burning into memory are **reflow** and **repaint**. A **reflow** re-runs layout because a geometry changed (an element got wider, text was added) — expensive, because moving one box can shove everything after it. A **repaint** redraws pixels without moving anything (you changed a colour) — cheaper. Some changes (like `transform` and `opacity`) can skip both and happen purely at the **composite** stage on the GPU — cheapest of all.",
