      "Slow operations (a network request, reading a file, a timer) don't run *on* this thread. The browser or Node hands them to background machinery (Web APIs / the OS), and when they finish, their callback is dropped into a queue. Your single thread never waits — it just gets notified later. That's **non-blocking** I/O, and it's the whole reason one thread can serve thousands of things at once.",
